data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":289,"item_id":"fc_03037c75de7a2c36016adb343fe24081919b105eac714cdbfa","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_input\\\":\\\"prior-candidate.json\\\",\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/2/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"If D=0, choose x₀∈E by H001. Projection F→I is onto and 1-Lipschitz. Mapping covers by this map cannot increase diameters, so dim_H(I)≤dim_H(F). Since I has nonempty interior in ℝ, the pinned interval dimension theorem gives dim_H(I)=1. Thus dim_H(F)≥1=D+1. For n=0, this branch handles the only possible E⊆ℝ⁰: E is the singleton, D=0, and no positive-diameter replacement is needed. Combine with the upper bound for n≥1.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/0/bindings\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Instantiate with X=ℝⁿ, s=E, and d equal to the NNReal coercion of the positive exponent used below dim_H(E).\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/1/bindings\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Instantiate with α=[0,1], β=ℕ, restricted Lebesgue measure, and f(i,t)=ρ_i^s times the indicator of J_i∩[0,1].\\\"}]}\"}"}

